+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the dimensionality of the network inputs/outputs. VRAM usage is a primary constraint, often mitigated by chunking rays [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images typically takes 250 epochs or ~100,000 iterations [S4, unofficial][S11, unofficial]. For a dataset of 199 down-sampled images, training with a batch size of 1024 results in ~7,800 iterations per epoch [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size, but larger batches hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batches cap iterations per second. A batch size of $2^{10}$ is often chosen as a tradeoff [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Stochastic optimization allows training on large datasets by sampling rays rather than rendering full images [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Rendering quality is limited by coarse voxel grid resolution and lack of advanced ray sampling in some implementations, resulting in blurry images [S17, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When optimizing a neural radiance field from multi-view images.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time inference is required without pre-training, or when camera poses are unknown and require joint optimization (use [Camera Pose and Parameter Optimization](7611-camera-pose-and-parameter-optimization.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the seminal NeRF paper (Mildenhall et al., 2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard paradigm of optimizing a coordinate MLP via stochastic gradient descent on ray samples. Subsequent works like HashNeRF [S7, unofficial], Mip-NeRF 360 [S1], and various regularization techniques have refined this loop, but the core structure of ray sampling, forward rendering, MSE loss, and Adam optimization remains consistent [S1][S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S7, unofficial][S8][S9, unofficial][S10, unofficial][S11, unofficial][S12][S13, unofficial][S14, unofficial][S15, unofficial][S16, unofficial][S17, unofficial][S18, unofficial].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the original NeRF paper by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard procedure of optimizing a coordinate MLP to minimize the MSE between rendered and observed RGB colors. Subsequent works like HashNeRF [S7, unofficial], HoloINR [S16, unofficial], and Mip-NeRF 360 [S1] have adapted this loop with different sampling strategies, architectures, and regularizers. It is widely used in computer vision and graphics for novel view synthesis [S1][S2, S3, S4, S5, S6, S7, unofficial][S8][S9, S10, S11, unofficial][S12][S13, S14, S15, S16, S17, S18, S19, unofficial][S20][S21, unofficial][S22, S23].
 
